  "background": "Job is wisdom literature set around a righteous man from the land of Uz. The narrator repeatedly calls Job blameless and upright before the speeches begin. Readers are shown a heavenly council that Job and his friends never see. In that scene “the accuser/adversary” challenges Job’s motives, and Job suffers catastrophic loss even though God later describes the attack as “without cause” (Job 2:3). The prologue therefore blocks the friends’ claim that Job’s suffering proves secret wickedness.\n\nMost of the book is poetic debate, not a list of statements that should all be repeated as doctrine. Job, Eliphaz, Bildad, Zophar, and Elihu speak from different perspectives, and God later says the three friends have not spoken rightly about Him as Job has. The divine speeches expand Job’s vision without giving him the hidden prologue as an explanation. The ending brings vindication and new gifts, but it does not turn children into replaceable units or promise every faithful sufferer doubled health, wealth, or family.",
